  {
    "id" : "asyncapi-3-payload-unsupported-schemaFormat",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Compatibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Disallow Unsupported schemaFormat in AsyncAPI Payloads",
    "description" : "This rule checks that the 'schemaFormat' property in AsyncAPI message payloads is either unspecified or set to the default supported values, as explicit schemaFormat values are not currently supported by Spectral for validation.",
    "explanation" : "# asyncapi-3-payload-unsupported-schemaFormat\n\nAsyncAPI can support various `schemaFormat` values. When unspecified, one of the following will be assumed:\n\napplication/vnd.aai.asyncapi;version=2.0.0\napplication/vnd.aai.asyncapi+json;version=2.0.0\napplication/vnd.aai.asyncapi+yaml;version=2.0.0\n\nAt this point, explicitly setting `schemaFormat` is not supported by Spectral, so if you use it this rule will emit an info message and skip validating the payload.\n\nOther formats such as OpenAPI Schema Object, JSON Schema Draft 07, and Avro will be added in various upcoming versions.\n\n**Recommended:** Yes",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "asyncapi-3-server-no-empty-variable",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Disallow Empty Variable Substitution in AsyncAPI Server Host and Pathname",
    "description" : "Ensures that server host and pathname in AsyncAPI definitions do not contain empty variable substitution patterns.",
    "explanation" : "# asyncapi-3-server-no-empty-variable\n\nServer host and pathname variable declarations cannot be empty, ex.`gigantic-server.com{}` is invalid.\n\n**Recommended:** Yes",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "asyncapi-3-server-not-example-com",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using example.com as Server Host",
    "description" : "This rule ensures that the server host in AsyncAPI specifications does not point to example.com.",
    "explanation" : "# asyncapi-3-server-not-example-com\n\nServer host should not point to example.com.\n\n**Recommended:** No",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "asyncapi-3-server-no-trailing-slash",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Disallow Trailing Slash in AsyncAPI Server Host",
    "description" : "This rule enforces that the server host in AsyncAPI specifications must not end with a trailing slash.",
    "explanation" : "# asyncapi-3-server-no-trailing-slash\n\nServer host should not have a trailing slash.\n\nSome tooling forgets to strip trailing slashes off when it's joining the `servers.host` with `channels`, and you can get awkward URLs like `mqtt://example.com//pets`. Best to just strip them off yourself.\n\n**Recommended:** Yes\n\n**Good Example**\n\n```yaml\nservers:\n  - host: mqtt://example.com\n```\n\n**Bad Example**\n\n```yaml\nservers:\n  - host: mqtt://example.com/\n  - host: mqtt://example.com/broker/\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "asyncapi-3-tag-description",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Documentation",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Tag Object Description",
    "description" : "Ensures that every tag object in an AsyncAPI document includes a descriptive \"description\" field.",
    "explanation" : "# asyncapi-3-tag-description\n\nTags alone are not very descriptive. Give folks a bit more information to work with.\n\n```yaml\ninfo:\n  tags:\n    - name: \"Aardvark\"\n      description: Funny-nosed pig-head raccoon.\n    - name: \"Badger\"\n      description: Angry short-legged omnivores.\n```\n\nIf your tags are business objects then you can use the term to explain them a bit. An 'Account' could be a user account, company information, bank account, potential sales lead, or anything. What is clear to the folks writing the document is probably not as clear to others.\n\n```yaml\ninfo:\n  tags:\n    - name: Invoice Items\n      description: |+\n        Giant long explanation about what this business concept is, because other people _might_ not have a clue!\n```\n\n**Recommended:** No",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "asyncapi-3-tags",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Documentation",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Require Non-Empty Tags Array in AsyncAPI Document",
    "description" : "Ensures that an AsyncAPI document includes a non-empty 'tags' array.",
    "explanation" : "# asyncapi-3-tags\n\nAsyncAPI object should have non-empty `tags` array.\n\nWhy? Well, you _can_ reference tags arbitrarily in operations, and definition is optional...\n\n```yaml\ninvoicedItems:\n  address: /invoices/{id}/items\n  tags:\n    - Invoice Items\n```\n\nDefining tags allows you to add more information like a `description`. For more information see [asyncapi-3-tag-description](#asyncapi-3-tag-description).\n\n**Recommended:** Yes",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "asyncapi-3-tags-alphabetical",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Alphabetical Order of AsyncAPI Tags",
    "description" : "Ensures that the tags array in an AsyncAPI object is sorted alphabetically by the 'name' property.",
    "explanation" : "# asyncapi-3-tags-alphabetical\n\nAsyncAPI object should have alphabetical `tags`. This will be sorted by the `name` property.\n\n**Recommended:** No\n\n**Bad Example**\n\n```yaml\ninfo:\n  tags:\n    - name: \"Badger\"\n    - name: \"Aardvark\"\n```\n\n**Good Example**\n\n```yaml\ninfo:\n  tags:\n    - name: \"Aardvark\"\n    - name: \"Badger\"\n```\n\n**Recommended:** No",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "asyncapi-3-tags-uniqueness",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Unique Tag Names",
    "description" : "Ensures that each tag in an AsyncAPI document has a unique name to avoid duplication.",
    "explanation" : "# asyncapi-3-tags-uniqueness\n\nTags must not have duplicate names (identifiers).\n\n**Recommended:** Yes\n\n**Bad Example**\n\n```yaml\ninfo:\n  tags:\n    - name: \"Badger\"\n    - name: \"Badger\"\n```\n\n**Good Example**\n\n```yaml\ninfo:\n  tags:\n    - name: \"Aardvark\"\n    - name: \"Badger\"\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
