  {
    "id" : "asyncapi-server-security",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "Auth",
    "title" : "Enforce Server Security Scheme Reference",
    "description" : "Servers must reference security schemes defined in the components.securitySchemes object.",
    "explanation" : "# asyncapi-server-security\n\nServer `security` values must match a scheme defined in the `components.securitySchemes` object. It also checks if there are `oauth2` scopes that have been defined for the given security.\n\n**Recommended:** Yes\n\n**Good Example**\n\n```yaml\nasyncapi: 2.0.0\nservers:\n  production:\n    url: test.mosquitto.org\n    security:\n      - petstore_auth: []\ncomponents:\n  securitySchemes:\n    petstore_auth: ...\n```\n\n**Bad Example**\n\n```yaml\nasyncapi: 2.0.0\nservers:\n  production:\n    url: test.mosquitto.org\n    security:\n      - not_defined: []\ncomponents:\n  securitySchemes:\n    petstore_auth: ...\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "asyncapi-server-variables",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Definition of Server Variables Without Redundancy",
    "description" : "Ensures that all server URL variables are defined in the server's variables object and that no redundant variables exist which are not part of the server address.",
    "explanation" : "# asyncapi-server-variables\n\nAll server URL variables should be defined in the `variables` object of the server. They should also not contain redundant variables that do not exist in the server address.\n\n**Recommended:** Yes",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "asyncapi-tag-description",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Documentation",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Tag Object Description",
    "description" : "Ensures that each tag object in an AsyncAPI document includes a descriptive \"description\" field.",
    "explanation" : "# asyncapi-tag-description\n\nTags alone are not very descriptive. Give folks a bit more information to work with.\n\n```yaml\ntags:\n  - name: \"Aardvark\"\n    description: Funny-nosed pig-head raccoon.\n  - name: \"Badger\"\n    description: Angry short-legged omnivores.\n```\n\nIf your tags are business objects then you can use the term to explain them a bit. An 'Account' could be a user account, company information, bank account, potential sales lead, or anything. What is clear to the folks writing the document is probably not as clear to others.\n\n```yaml\ntags:\n  - name: Invoice Items\n    description: |+\n      Giant long explanation about what this business concept is, because other people _might_ not have a clue!\n```\n\n**Recommended:** No",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "asyncapi-tags",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Documentation",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Non-Empty Tags Array in AsyncAPI Object",
    "description" : "Ensures that the AsyncAPI root object contains a non-empty 'tags' array.",
    "explanation" : "# asyncapi-tags\n\nAsyncAPI root object should have non-empty `tags` array.\n\nWhy? Well, you _can_ reference tags arbitrarily in operations, and definition is optional...\n\n```yaml\n/invoices/{id}/items:\n  tags:\n    - Invoice Items\n```\n\nDefining tags allows you to add more information like a `description`. For more information see [asyncapi-tag-description](#asyncapi-tag-description).\n\n**Recommended:** Yes",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "asyncapi-tags-alphabetical",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Alphabetical Order of AsyncAPI Tags",
    "description" : "Ensures that the tags in an AsyncAPI object are ordered alphabetically by their name property.",
    "explanation" : "# asyncapi-tags-alphabetical\n\nAsyncAPI object should have alphabetical `tags`. This will be sorted by the `name` property.\n\n**Recommended:** No\n\n**Bad Example**\n\n```yaml\ntags:\n  - name: \"Badger\"\n  - name: \"Aardvark\"\n```\n\n**Good Example**\n\n```yaml\ntags:\n  - name: \"Aardvark\"\n  - name: \"Badger\"\n```\n\n**Recommended:** No",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "asyncapi-tags-uniqueness",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Unique Tag Names",
    "description" : "Ensures that each tag in an AsyncAPI document has a unique name to avoid duplication.",
    "explanation" : "# asyncapi-tags-uniqueness\n\nTags must not have duplicate names (identifiers).\n\n**Recommended:** Yes\n\n**Bad Example**\n\n```yaml\ntags:\n  - name: \"Badger\"\n  - name: \"Badger\"\n```\n\n**Good Example**\n\n```yaml\ntags:\n  - name: \"Aardvark\"\n  - name: \"Badger\"\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "asyncapi-unused-components-schema",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Detect Potentially Unused Components Schema",
    "description" : "Detects potentially unused reusable schema components in AsyncAPI specifications.",
    "explanation" : "# asyncapi-unused-components-schema\n\nPotential unused reusable `schema` entry has been detected.\n\n<!-- theme: warning -->\n\n_Warning:_ This rule may identify false positives when linting a specification\nthat acts as a library (a container storing reusable objects, leveraged by other\nspecifications that reference those objects).\n\n**Recommended:** Yes",
