  {
    "id" : "oas3-valid-media-example",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Validity of Examples Against Their Defined Schema",
    "description" : "Ensure that example values in OpenAPI Media Type objects conform to their defined schema types and constraints.",
    "explanation" : "# oas3-valid-media-example\n\nExamples must be valid against their defined schema. This rule is applied to Media Type objects.\n\n**Recommended:** Yes\n\nFor example, if you have a Pet object with an `id` property as type `integer`, and `name` and `petType` properties as type `string`, the examples properties type should match the schema:\n\n```yaml\nschemas:\n  Pet:\n    title: Pet\n    type: object\n    properties:\n      id:\n        type: integer\n      name:\n        type: string\n      petType:\n        type: string\n    required:\n      - id\n      - name\n      - petType\n```\n\n**Good Example**\n\n```yaml\npaths:\n  '/pet/{petId}':\n    get:\n      ...\n      responses:\n        '200':\n          description: Pet Found\n          schema:\n            $ref: '#/definitions/Pet'\n          examples:\n            Get Pet Bubbles:\n              id: 123\n              name: 'Bubbles'\n              petType: 'dog'\n```\n\n**Bad Example**\n\nThis would throw an error since `petType` is an `integer`, not a `string`.\n\n```yaml\npaths:\n  '/pet/{petId}':\n    get:\n      ...\n      responses:\n        '200':\n          description: Pet Found\n          schema:\n            $ref: '#/definitions/Pet'\n          examples:\n            Get Pet Bubbles:\n              id: 123\n              name: 'Bubbles'\n              petType: 123\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "oas3-valid-schema-example",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Valid Examples Against Schema",
    "description" : "Ensures that example values in OpenAPI 3 schema definitions conform to their declared data types and schema constraints.",
    "explanation" : "# oas3-valid-schema-example\n\nExamples must be valid against their defined schema. This rule is applied to Schema objects.\n\n**Recommended:** Yes\n\n**Good Example**\n\n```yaml\nschemas:\n  Pet:\n    title: Pet\n    type: object\n    properties:\n      id:\n        type: integer\n        example: 123\n      name:\n        type: string\n        example: Bubbles\n      petType:\n        type: string\n        example: dog\n    required:\n      - id\n      - name\n      - petType\n```\n\n**Bad Example**\n\nThis would throw an error since the example value for `petType` is an `integer`, not a `string`.\n\n```yaml\nschemas:\n  Pet:\n    title: Pet\n    type: object\n    properties:\n      id:\n        type: integer\n        example: 123\n      name:\n        type: string\n        example: Bubbles\n      petType:\n        type: string\n        example: 123\n    required:\n      - name\n      - petType\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "openapi-tags",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Documentation",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Non-Empty Tags Array in OpenAPI Object",
    "description" : "Ensures that the OpenAPI object contains a non-empty \"tags\" array.",
    "explanation" : "# openapi-tags\n\nOpenAPI object should have non-empty `tags` array.\n\nWhy? Well, you _can_ reference tags arbitrarily in operations, and definition is optional...\n\n```yaml\n/invoices/{id}/items:\n  get:\n    tags:\n      - Invoice Items\n```\n\nDefining tags allows you to add more information like a `description`. For more information see [tag-description](#tag-description).",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "openapi-tags-alphabetical",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Alphabetical Order of OpenAPI Tags",
    "description" : "Ensures that the tags array in an OpenAPI object is sorted alphabetically by the name property.",
    "explanation" : "# openapi-tags-alphabetical\n\nOpenAPI object should have alphabetical `tags`. This will be sorted by the `name` property.\n\n**Recommended:** No\n\n**Bad Example**\n\n```yaml\ntags:\n  - name: \"Badger\"\n  - name: \"Aardvark\"\n```\n\n**Good Example**\n\n```yaml\ntags:\n  - name: \"Aardvark\"\n  - name: \"Badger\"\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "openapi-tags-uniqueness",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Unique Tag Names in OpenAPI",
    "description" : "Ensures that each tag in the OpenAPI specification has a unique name to avoid duplication.",
    "explanation" : "# openapi-tags-uniqueness\n\nOpenAPI object must not have duplicated tag names (identifiers).\n\n**Recommended:** Yes\n\n**Bad Example**\n\n```yaml\ntags:\n  - name: \"Badger\"\n  - name: \"Badger\"\n```\n\n**Good Example**\n\n```yaml\ntags:\n  - name: \"Aardvark\"\n  - name: \"Badger\"\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "operation-description",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Documentation",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Require Non-Empty Operation Description",
    "description" : "Ensures that each operation has a description property that is present and not empty.",
    "explanation" : "# operation-description\n\n**Recommended:** Yes",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
