    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "operation-operationId",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Operation to Have operationId",
    "description" : "Ensures that every API operation has a unique \"operationId\" defined.",
    "explanation" : "# operation-operationId\n\nThis operation ID is essentially a reference for the operation, which can be used to visually suggest a connection to other operations. This is like some theoretical static HATEOAS-style referencing, but it's also used for the URL in some documentation systems.\n\nMake the value `lower-hyphen-case`, and try and think of a name for the action which does not relate to the HTTP message. Base it off the actual action being performed. `create-polygon`? `search-by-polygon`? `filter-companies`?\n\n**Recommended:** Yes",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "operation-operationId-unique",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Unique operationId for Every Operation",
    "description" : "Ensures that every operation in the API specification has a unique operationId.",
    "explanation" : "# operation-operationId-unique\n\nEvery operation must have a unique `operationId`.\n\nWhy? A lot of documentation systems use this as an identifier, some SDK generators convert them to a method name, among other things.\n\n**Recommended:** Yes\n\n**Bad Example**\n\n```yaml\npaths:\n  /pet:\n    patch:\n      operationId: \"update-pet\"\n      responses:\n        200:\n          description: ok\n    put:\n      operationId: \"update-pet\"\n      responses:\n        200:\n          description: ok\n```\n\n**Good Example**\n\n```yaml\npaths:\n  /pet:\n    patch:\n      operationId: \"update-pet\"\n      responses:\n        200:\n          description: ok\n    put:\n      operationId: \"replace-pet\"\n      responses:\n        200:\n          description: ok\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "operation-operationId-valid-in-url",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce operationId to be URL-safe",
    "description" : "Ensures that the operationId in API definitions contains only URL-safe characters to avoid issues in documentation systems that use operationId as unique URLs.",
    "explanation" : "# operation-operationId-valid-in-url\n\nSeeing as operationId is often used for unique URLs in documentation systems, it's a good idea to avoid non-URL-safe characters.\n\n**Recommended:** Yes\n\n**Bad Example**\n\n```yaml\npaths:\n  /pets:\n    get:\n      operationId: get cats\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "operation-parameters",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Unique and Non-Repeating Operation Parameters",
    "description" : "Ensures that each operation's parameters are unique by name and location, and comply with OpenAPI specifications regarding parameter usage.",
    "explanation" : "# operation-parameters\n\nOperation parameters are unique and non-repeating.\n\n1. Operations must have unique `name` + `in` parameters.\n2. Operation cannot have both `in: body` and `in: formData` parameters. (OpenAPI v2.0)\n3. Operation must have only one `in: body` parameter. (OpenAPI v2.0)\n\n**Recommended:** Yes",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "operation-singular-tag",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Documentation",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Single Tag per Operation",
    "description" : "Ensures that each operation in an API specification has no more than one tag assigned to it.",
    "explanation" : "# operation-singular-tag\n\nUse just one tag for an operation, which is helpful for some documentation systems which use tags to avoid duplicate content.\n\n**Recommended:** No",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "operation-success-response",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Operation Has At Least One Success Response",
    "description" : "Ensures that every API operation defines at least one successful HTTP response status code in the 2xx or 3xx range.",
    "explanation" : "# operation-success-response\n\nOperation must have at least one `2xx` or `3xx` response. Any API operation (endpoint) can fail, but presumably, it is also meant to do something constructive at some point. If you forget to write out a success case for this API, then this rule will let you know.\n\n**Recommended:** Yes\n\n**Bad Example**\n\n```yaml\npaths:\n  /path:\n    get:\n      responses:\n        418:\n          description: teapot\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
